-- title:
--   Theorem 4.1 — the accelerated proximal point method has fixed-point residual $\|x_i-y_{i-1}\|^2\le R^2/i^2$
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space, let $M\in\mathcal M(\mathcal H)$ be a maximally monotone operator, let $\lambda>0$, and let $x_0,y_0,x_1,y_1,\dots\in\mathcal H$ be generated by the proposed accelerated proximal point method
--   $$
--   x_0=y_0=y_{-1},\qquad x_{i+1}=J_{\lambda M}(y_i),\qquad y_{i+1}=x_{i+1}+\frac{i}{i+2}(x_{i+1}-x_i)-\frac{i}{i+2}(x_i-y_{i-1}).
--   $$
--   Assume $\|x_0-x_*\|\le R$ for a constant $R>0$ and some $x_*\in X_*(M)=\{x:0\in Mx\}$. Then for every $i\ge1$,
--   $$
--   \|x_i-y_{i-1}\|^2\ \le\ \frac{R^2}{i^2}.
--   $$
--   Here $x_i-y_{i-1}=J_{\lambda M}(y_{i-1})-y_{i-1}$ is the fixed-point residual of the resolvent at $y_{i-1}$. The rate $O(1/i^2)$ improves the $O(1/i)$ rate of the plain proximal point method, and it holds in every real Hilbert space, for every step size and every maximally monotone operator.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The step size $\lambda$ is written `lam`. The resolvent step is encoded relationally as $\lambda^{-1}(y_i-x_{i+1})\in Mx_{i+1}$, i.e. $y_i\in(I+\lambda M)x_{i+1}$; for monotone $M$ and $\lambda>0$ the resolvent is single-valued, so the inclusion determines $x_{i+1}=J_{\lambda M}(y_i)$, and for maximally monotone $M$ it is defined everywhere (Minty), so runs exist from every start. The sequences are indexed by `ℕ`; $y_{-1}=y_0$ is `y (0 - 1) = y 0` by natural-number subtraction. $i$ is cast to $\mathbb R$ in $R^2/i^2$.
-- source:
--   Kim, Accelerated proximal point method for maximally monotone operators, arXiv:1905.05149v4, p. 10, Theorem 4.1, Eq. (27)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AccelPPM_FPR_IsMaximalMonotone
import Definitions.Def_AccelPPM_FPR_IsAccelPPMSeq

namespace AccelPPM.FPR

/-- **Theorem 4.1** (Kim, arXiv:1905.05149v4, p. 10). Let `M` be a maximally monotone operator on
a real Hilbert space `H`, let `λ > 0`, and let `x₀, y₀, x₁, y₁, … ∈ H` be generated by the proposed
accelerated proximal point method. Assume `‖x₀ − x_*‖ ≤ R` for a constant `R > 0` and some
`x_* ∈ X_*(M)`. Then for every `i ≥ 1`,
`‖x_i − y_{i−1}‖² ≤ R² / i²`. -/
theorem theorem4_1_fixed_point_residual {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H]
    [InnerProductSpace ℝ H] [CompleteSpace H]
    (M : H → Set H) (hM : IsMaximalMonotone M) (lam : ℝ) (hlam : 0 < lam)
    (x y : ℕ → H) (hseq : IsAccelPPMSeq M lam x y)
    (xstar : H) (hxstar : xstar ∈ zeroSet M) (R : ℝ) (hR : 0 < R)
    (hx0 : ‖x 0 - xstar‖ ≤ R) :
    ∀ i : ℕ, 1 ≤ i → ‖x i - y (i - 1)‖ ^ 2 ≤ R ^ 2 / (i : ℝ) ^ 2 := by sorry

end AccelPPM.FPR
